      "<p>Long Island City closets are not blank boxes. New towers give you a walk-in with a bulkhead, a sprinkler head, and a door that barely clears a king bed. Older walk-ups give you a reach-in with out-of-square plaster and a steam riser stealing six inches. Catalog wire systems assume a perfect rectangle and a loading dock. Your building does not.</p>",
      "<p>Green Cabinets NY builds custom closet systems by appointment in Brooklyn, Manhattan, and Queens &mdash; including Long Island City condos, co-ops, and rentals. We are home-based in Bushwick, not a walk-in showroom at 10 Montieth. This post deepens our <a href=\"https://greencabinetsny.com/blog/custom-closet-millwork-brooklyn\">Brooklyn closet millwork</a> and <a href=\"https://greencabinetsny.com/blog/reach-in-closet-millwork-manhattan-coops\">Manhattan reach-in closets</a> for LIC specifically. Related: <a href=\"https://greencabinetsny.com/custom-kitchen-cabinets-queens\">Queens kitchens</a>, <a href=\"https://greencabinetsny.com/blog/custom-kitchen-cabinets-astoria-queens\">Astoria kitchens</a>, and <a href=\"https://greencabinetsny.com/case-studies\">case studies</a>.</p>",
      "<h2>What a custom closet system means in LIC</h2>",
      "<p>A custom closet system is furniture-grade casework measured to your actual niche &mdash; not a tension-pole kit from a big-box aisle:</p>",
      "<ul>",
      "<li><strong>Walk-in closets</strong> with double hang, shoe shelves, drawers, and a center island when the floor plate allows</li>",
      "<li><strong>Reach-in closets</strong> with floor-to-ceiling panels that hide bulkheads and maximize hang height</li>",
      "<li><strong>Entry / coat closets</strong> sized for jackets, bags, and vacuum storage without blocking the door swing</li>",
      "<li><strong>Secondary storage</strong> &mdash; linen niches, entry coat closets, and laundry-adjacent niches with the same hardware as the primary suite</li>",
      "</ul>",
      "<p>We do not invent awards or claim design renders are finished installs. Previews are layout tools. Finished photos live on <a href=\"https://greencabinetsny.com/case-studies\">case studies</a> when a client allows them. For vanity-scale adjacent baths, the <a href=\"https://greencabinetsny.com/designer\">online designer</a> helps map the next room.</p>",
      "<h2>Why stock closet kits fail in Long Island City</h2>",
      "<p>LIC housing stock splits two ways: glass-tower condos along the waterfront and Queens Plaza, and older brick walk-ups and small co-ops toward Court Square and Hunters Point.</p>",
      "<p><strong>New towers.</strong> Cleaner slabs, but shallow walk-ins, sprinkler clearances, HVAC returns in the ceiling, and freight elevators that need a reservation and a <a href=\"https://greencabinetsny.com/blog/certificate-of-insurance-kitchen-remodel-nyc\">Certificate of Insurance</a>. A kit that arrives in 84-inch panels will not clear a 78-inch freight cab.</p>",
      "<p><strong>Older buildings.</strong> Out-of-square walls, plaster that will not hold a cheap rail screw, and stair-only buildings with no freight &mdash; same discipline as <a href=\"https://greencabinetsny.com/blog/freight-elevator-kitchen-cabinet-delivery-nyc\">freight elevator delivery</a> even when the path is four flights and a shared landing.</p>",
      "<p>A custom system is measured on site: width at floor and ceiling, depth past the door stop, hang height under the bulkhead, and panel sizes that fit the delivery path. Fillers and scribe strips are designed in &mdash; not improvised with shims on install day.</p>",
      "<h2>Layout moves that make an LIC closet usable</h2>",
      "<p><strong>Honest hang depth.</strong> Standard hang needs about 24 inches clear. Many LIC walk-ins are closer to 20&ndash;22 inches after the door swing. We adjust rod depth and use pull-out rods or double hang where full depth will not fit.</p>",
      "<p><strong>Drawers where you reach.</strong> Soft-close drawers beat deep shelves you never see the back of &mdash; see <a href=\"https://greencabinetsny.com/blog/soft-close-blum-vs-hettich-nyc\">Blum vs Hettich soft-close</a> and <a href=\"https://greencabinetsny.com/blog/dovetail-drawer-boxes-nyc-kitchens\">dovetail drawer boxes</a> when you want furniture-grade boxes behind a painted or veneer face.</p>",
      "<p><strong>Bulkhead and pipe workarounds.</strong> Ceiling drops and risers are normal. We notch panels and stop hang short of the obstruction instead of fighting it with a stock tower that sits crooked.</p>",
      "<p><strong>Finish that survives humidity.</strong> Painted MDF scuffs; durable laminate and wood-look systems we already use (Tafisa, Egger, Shinnoki, Wilsonart, AGT) wipe clean. Match kitchen language via <a href=\"https://greencabinetsny.com/wood-species\">wood species</a> and <a href=\"https://greencabinetsny.com/blog/shaker-vs-slim-shaker-nyc\">shaker vs slim shaker</a> when the closet opens into a finished bedroom.</p>",
      "<h2>Condo boards, COI, and occupied-unit logistics</h2>",
      "<p>LIC condos and co-ops usually treat closet millwork like any other alteration &mdash; quieter than a full kitchen gut, but still under house rules:</p>",
