      "<p>Park Slope kitchens were not drawn for a 36-inch stock box. Parlor floors run long and narrow toward the rear yard. Garden levels sit lower, with shorter ceilings and a chimney breast that steals a cabinet bay. Plaster walls lean. A catalog pack that looked fine in a showroom leaves fillers and a corner that never closes clean.</p>",
      "<p>Green Cabinets NY designs and installs custom kitchen cabinets for Park Slope brownstones, co-ops, and condos by appointment. We are home-based in Bushwick &mdash; not a walk-in shop. Measure and sample visits happen in your kitchen. See <a href=\"https://greencabinetsny.com/custom-kitchen-cabinets-park-slope\">custom kitchen cabinets in Park Slope</a>, the <a href=\"https://greencabinetsny.com/custom-kitchen-cabinets-brooklyn\">Brooklyn kitchen cabinets</a> map, and our earlier <a href=\"https://greencabinetsny.com/blog/brooklyn-brownstone-kitchen-millwork\">Brooklyn brownstone millwork</a> overview. This article goes deeper on Park Slope alone: floor type, building rules, freight path, and how we spec the boxes.</p>",
      "<h2>Parlor floor vs garden floor in Park Slope</h2>",
      "<p><strong>Parlor floor.</strong> Higher ceilings, often 10&ndash;11 feet after a gut or a careful remodel. The kitchen usually sits at the rear, sometimes opened to dining or a deck. Uppers can climb. A glazed display row or glass uppers make sense when you want light from the garden doors. Crown and face frames can pick up the language of original cornice without pretending the cabinets are 1890 originals.</p>",
      "<p><strong>Garden floor.</strong> Lower headroom, more bulkheads, more pipe and stack noise from upstairs. A 42-inch upper that fits the parlor may not fit here. We measure finished clear height after soffits and beams, then cut the box plan to that opening. Drawer banks and pantry towers often beat tall uppers when the ceiling fights you.</p>",
      "<p><strong>North Slope vs South Slope.</strong> Same millwork shop, different freight habits. Seventh Avenue and Prospect Park West often mean co-op rules and a freight elevator when the building has one. Brownstone blocks east of Fifth still lean on stair carry. We cover 11215 and 11217 &mdash; North Slope, South Slope, and Prospect Park West.</p>",
      "<h2>Why stock cabinets fail in a Park Slope brownstone</h2>",
      "<ul>",
      "<li>Walls and floors out of square after a century of settlement</li>",
      "<li>Chimney breasts and radiator niches that eat fixed cabinet modules</li>",
      "<li>Sink stacks that pin the wet wall where a stock layout wants an island</li>",
      "<li>Door swings into a narrow galley that already fights the fridge</li>",
      "<li>Landmark-adjacent interiors where original moldings deserve a clean scribe, not a filler strip</li>",
      "</ul>",
      "<p>Custom millwork starts from the clear opening. We scribe fillers on site so the run looks built into the plaster, not parked against it. Door styles that show up often in Park Slope: classic <a href=\"https://greencabinetsny.com/blog/shaker-vs-slim-shaker-nyc\">shaker and slim shaker</a>, sometimes slab in a modernized rear addition. Finish and species notes live in the <a href=\"https://greencabinetsny.com/wood-species\">wood species guide</a> and our <a href=\"https://greencabinetsny.com/blog/wood-species-kitchen-cabinets-nyc\">species article</a>.</p>",
      "<h2>Landmarks, moldings, and matching trim</h2>",
