      "<p>A media wall in New York is rarely blank drywall with a TV on two screws. Living rooms share walls with neighbors, co-ops ask about mounting, and cables still have to disappear. Custom media wall millwork turns that wall into a planned elevation: TV niche, storage, speaker bays, and cable paths drawn before panels leave the shop.</p>",
      "<p>Green Cabinets NY designs and installs TV built-ins and media wall millwork by appointment in Brooklyn, Manhattan, and Queens. We are home-based in Bushwick &mdash; not a walk-in shop. Measure and sample visits happen in your apartment. Kitchen cabinetry lives on our <a href=\"/custom-kitchen-cabinets-brooklyn\">Brooklyn</a>, <a href=\"/custom-kitchen-cabinets-manhattan\">Manhattan</a>, and <a href=\"/custom-kitchen-cabinets-queens\">Queens</a> pages; this post is about the living-room wall that holds the screen.</p>",
      "<h2>What a media wall means in an NYC apartment</h2>",
      "<p>In a Williamsburg loft or Park Slope parlor, a media wall might span a full party wall &mdash; floor-to-ceiling cabinets flanking a centered TV recess, with open shelves and closed bases for AV gear. In a Manhattan studio or Queens one-bedroom, it is often shorter: a floating ledge, slim towers, or a low console built to the exact wall width.</p>",
      "<p>We treat it as millwork, not furniture. Boxes are scribed to out-of-plumb plaster, anchored to structure, and finished to match or contrast the room. Open shelves, closed doors, and a shallow AV bay for a soundbar or receiver are common. When a fireplace already exists, surrounds can wrap the same elevation so TV and mantel read as one composition.</p>",
      "<h2>Cable management before the finish goes on</h2>",
      "<p>Ugly cable runs kill a nice wall. We plan chase space behind panels, grommets at the TV niche, and a ventilated AV cabinet for a power strip and HDMI switch. Rough-in first: confirm outlets, low-voltage paths, and whether the building allows in-wall HDMI or only raceways tucked inside the millwork. We coordinate with your electrician when wiring is outside our scope &mdash; we do not invent electrical work or replace licensed trades. Floor-to-ceiling walls can hide vertical chases beside the TV bay so nothing drapes the cabinet face.</p>",
      "<h2>Floating shelves vs floor-to-ceiling</h2>",
      "<h3>Floating / low console</h3>",
      "<p>Best in tight living rooms where a full wall would shrink seating. A low built-in holds remotes, consoles, and records without blocking windows or radiators. Depth is often 14&ndash;18 inches.</p>",
      "<h3>Floor-to-ceiling</h3>",
      "<p>Uses the full height of an 8- or 9-foot ceiling for books, display, and closed storage. The TV sits in a recessed niche at eye height with shelves above and drawers or doors below. Soft-close hardware matters next to a sofa.</p>",
      "<h3>Hybrid</h3>",
      "<p>Tall towers flanking a lower center console &mdash; common on brownstone chimney breasts that already break the wall into three zones. Speaker niches and fabric-backed bays can sit flush with the millwork. We leave clearances you specify; we do not size acoustic systems ourselves.</p>",
      "<h2>Co-op and condo TV mounting &mdash; awareness, not legal advice</h2>",
